WEBVTT

00:00:06.285 --> 00:00:09.915
We will save a small roster, add text, and read it

00:00:09.915 --> 00:00:10.278
back.

00:00:10.278 --> 00:00:12.964
File I O means file input and output.

00:00:12.964 --> 00:00:15.069
A path represents a location.

00:00:15.069 --> 00:00:17.756
Naming a path does not create a file.

00:00:17.756 --> 00:00:21.385
Our practice file will stay inside a newly created

00:00:21.385 --> 00:00:22.910
directory, or folder.

00:00:45.761 --> 00:00:49.193
An absolute path starts from the file system root.

00:00:49.193 --> 00:00:51.183
A relative path needs a base.

00:00:51.183 --> 00:00:54.546
Here, resolve combines the new directory with the

00:00:54.546 --> 00:00:56.605
relative name roster dot text.

00:00:56.605 --> 00:00:59.762
The temporary directory is a distinct practice

00:00:59.762 --> 00:01:00.380
location.

00:01:00.380 --> 00:01:03.743
It is not automatically deleted when this program

00:01:03.743 --> 00:01:04.086
ends.

00:01:04.388 --> 00:01:07.794
Character encoding is the rule for turning text into

00:01:07.794 --> 00:01:09.236
stored bytes and back.

00:01:09.236 --> 00:01:11.201
Both directions use UTF eight.

00:01:11.201 --> 00:01:14.476
By default, writeString creates a file or replaces

00:01:14.476 --> 00:01:15.655
existing contents.

00:01:15.655 --> 00:01:18.079
APPEND adds text to an existing file.

00:01:18.079 --> 00:01:20.176
These calls do not add newlines.

00:01:20.176 --> 00:01:21.748
The strings supply them.

00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:25.220
readString brings the whole small file into a String.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:27.840
These helpers close their own resources.

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:30.788
The catch handles an input or output failure.

00:01:30.863 --> 00:01:34.137
Predict every output line, including the character

00:01:34.137 --> 00:01:34.530
count.

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:37.543
Remember to count each newline in the strings.

00:01:37.543 --> 00:01:38.263
Pause here.

00:01:43.056 --> 00:01:46.475
The output shows Maya, Luis, and Nora on separate

00:01:46.475 --> 00:01:48.638
lines, then fifteen characters.

00:01:48.638 --> 00:01:52.266
Each name has four characters, and each newline adds

00:01:52.266 --> 00:01:52.545
one.

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:55.684
The appended text followed the existing text.

00:01:55.684 --> 00:01:59.382
String length is not a general measure of stored file

00:01:59.382 --> 00:01:59.731
size.

00:02:00.057 --> 00:02:03.468
Remove only the APPEND option from the second write,

00:02:03.468 --> 00:02:06.223
keeping the file path, text, and encoding.

00:02:06.223 --> 00:02:09.503
Predict the new file contents and character count,

00:02:09.503 --> 00:02:10.881
then run the program.

00:02:10.881 --> 00:02:13.964
What does this reveal about writing again to an

00:02:13.964 --> 00:02:14.882
existing file?
